<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Resources on Governor Richard Howell</title><link>https://govhowell.org/resources/</link><description>Recent content in Resources on Governor Richard Howell</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Thu, 27 Feb 2020 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://govhowell.org/resources/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Contact Us</title><link>https://govhowell.org/resources/contact/</link><pubDate>Thu, 27 Feb 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://govhowell.org/resources/contact/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="we-would-love-to-hear-from-you"&gt;We Would Love to Hear from You&lt;a class="anchor" href="#we-would-love-to-hear-from-you"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This site is a labor of love, built by Richard Howell&amp;rsquo;s descendants to preserve and share the story of a man who served his country as soldier, spy, statesman, and poet. We have been researching the Howell family for over ten years, and some of our best discoveries have come from people just like you.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you have information, a family connection, a document, or even a question, please reach out. Every lead matters.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Howell Homestead</title><link>https://govhowell.org/resources/homestead/</link><pubDate>Mon, 02 Mar 2015 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://govhowell.org/resources/homestead/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://govhowell.org/images/DSC00268.jpg"&gt;&lt;img src="https://govhowell.org/images/DSC00268.jpg" alt="Howell Homestead as it looked in 2010" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Howell Homestead as it looked in 2010&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://govhowell.org/images/111985pv.jpg"&gt;&lt;img src="https://govhowell.org/images/111985pv.jpg" alt="Howell Homestead, 1934" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ebenezer Howell&lt;/strong&gt;, Richard Howell&amp;rsquo;s father, removed from Newark, Delaware in 1769 and 4 years later built this house. Richard and his twin brother Lewis were 19 years old when they moved into the house.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At the time of the homestead survey there were initials &amp;ldquo;RH 1773&amp;rdquo; and &amp;ldquo;LH&amp;rdquo; carved next to the rear doorway. A year later after moving into this house on December 22, 1774, Richard and Lewis Howell participated in the &lt;a href="https://govhowell.org/events/"&gt;Greenwich Tea Burning&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;sup id="fnref:1"&gt;&lt;a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref"&gt;1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; They used their father&amp;rsquo;s house as a staging area to gather with other tea burners to plan their night&amp;rsquo;s activities.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Documents</title><link>https://govhowell.org/resources/documents/</link><pubDate>Sat, 28 Feb 2015 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://govhowell.org/resources/documents/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Here is a list of documents we have collected over the years. All documents are in PDF format.&lt;sup id="fnref:1"&gt;&lt;a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref"&gt;1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
